course: geography
lesson: 59
title: Coal, oil and gas
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Coal, oil and natural gas are called fossil fuels because they are made from living things that died millions of years ago. When we burn them we release sunlight that ancient plants caught long before the dinosaurs.
explain: Coal is squashed swamp forest, mostly from the Carboniferous period, about 360-300 million years ago. Bogs today show the first step: peat, partly rotted plants that people in Ireland and Scotland cut and burned on their fires.
explain: Oil and gas come from tiny sea plankton buried in mud and slowly cooked by the heat underground. Coal powered the Industrial Revolution from the late 1700s, but miners paid dearly: explosions, roof falls and lung disease, and in Britain even young children worked underground until 1842.
explain: Burning fossil fuels gives off carbon dioxide, the main cause of today's global warming, and smoke that harms people's lungs. They took millions of years to form and we are using them up in centuries, so countries are turning to the sun and wind; Britain closed its last coal power station in 2024.
fact: Coal, oil and natural gas are fossil fuels made from the remains of plants and tiny sea creatures buried millions of years ago.
fact: Coal formed mostly from swamp forests of the Carboniferous period, about 360-300 million years ago.
fact: Oil and gas formed from tiny sea plankton buried in mud and cooked slowly underground.
fact: Coal powered the Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the late 1700s.
fact: Coal mining was dangerous: explosions, roof falls and lung disease killed many miners.
fact: Children worked in British coal mines until a law of 1842 banned children under ten from working underground.
fact: Burning fossil fuels releases carbon dioxide, the main cause of today's global warming.
fact: Burning coal and oil also pollutes the air with soot and gases that cause millions of early deaths each year.
fact: Oil spills poison seas and coasts and kill seabirds, fish and other wildlife.
fact: Fossil fuels are non-renewable: they took millions of years to form and we are using them up in centuries.
fact: Countries are switching to solar, wind and other clean energy, which burn no fuel and give off no carbon dioxide while they work, and Britain closed its last coal power station in 2024.
fact: Peat is partly rotted plant matter in bogs, the first step towards coal, and was burned for fuel in Ireland and Scotland.
exercise: What are fossil fuels made from? | Remains of plants and tiny sea creatures buried millions of years ago | Dinosaur bones melted down by the heat of volcanoes deep under the ground | Rocks from the Moon | Rainwater trapped underground
exercise: Which three are fossil fuels? | Coal, oil and natural gas | Wind, sunlight and running water | Wood, straw and old newspaper | Iron, copper and tin from mines
exercise: What did most coal form from? | Swamp forests of the Carboniferous period | Dinosaurs that fell into lakes and sank into the mud | Desert sand pressed together | Volcanic ash on mountains
exercise: When was the Carboniferous period? | About 360-300 million years ago | About 3,600-3,000 years ago, in the Bronze Age | About 36-30 million years ago | About 3.6-3 billion years ago
exercise: What did oil and gas form from? | Tiny sea plankton buried in mud and cooked underground | Coal that melted in the sun | Whales that sank to the deep sea floor and slowly rotted there | Rain that soaked into sand
exercise: What did coal power? | The Industrial Revolution, from the late 1700s | The building of the great pyramids in ancient Egypt | The Roman roads | The first farms in the Stone Age
exercise: Where did the Industrial Revolution begin? | Britain | China | Brazil | Australia
exercise: Why was coal mining dangerous? | Explosions, roof falls and lung disease killed many miners | Coal is poisonous to touch, so miners' hands slowly went black and died | Mines were full of wild animals | Coal often flew out of the walls
exercise: Until when did children work underground in British coal mines? | Until a law of 1842 | Until the year 1642 or so | Until 1942, during the war | Until 2012, quite recently
exercise: What does burning fossil fuels release that warms the planet? | Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Helium | Pure water only
exercise: How does burning coal and oil harm people's health? | Soot and gases pollute the air and cause millions of early deaths | It makes the air far too clean and thin for people to breathe it properly | It turns rain into sugar | It has no effect on health
exercise: What harm do oil spills do? | They poison seas and coasts and kill wildlife | They make the fish grow bigger and fatter | They clean the beaches | They turn the salty sea water fresh enough to drink
exercise: Why are fossil fuels called non-renewable? | They took millions of years to form and we use them up in centuries | They grow back every single year, but much too slowly for us to keep up | They can only be used once a year | They are made in factories
exercise: When did Britain close its last coal power station? | 2024 | In 1924 | In 1842 | In 2050
exercise: What is peat? | Partly rotted plant matter in bogs, the first step towards coal | A kind of hard black rock from volcanoes | Oil that has dried out in the hot sun on a beach, leaving a crust | A rock made of seashells
exercise: Where was peat burned for fuel? | Ireland and Scotland | Egypt and Libya in Africa | Frozen Antarctica's coast | The Sahara desert sands
test: "oil comes from dead dinosaurs" — it says so on a cartoon dinosaur at a petrol station. What is true? | Oil comes mostly from tiny sea plankton, not dinosaurs | Yes, oil is melted dinosaurs, and that's exactly why it is black | Oil comes from volcanoes only | Oil comes from tree sap
test: A lump of coal shows the print of a fern leaf. Why? | Coal formed from ancient swamp forests | Someone drew the fern on it with a pencil | Ferns grow on coal today | Coal is made of fossil fish
test: grandad: "my dad went down the pit at 14, bloody dangerous it was." What dangers did miners face? | Explosions, roof falls and lung disease | Sunburn and seasickness | Bee stings and snake bites in the dark tunnels | Too much fresh air
test: What would happen if we kept burning fossil fuels at today's rate for centuries? | They would run out, and the warming would get much worse | They would grow back faster than we can ever use them up | The climate would cool down | The air would get cleaner
test: Jack: "why can't we just make more coal when it runs out?" What's the answer? | It takes millions of years to form | It's against the law to make coal at home | Coal is made only on the Moon, far away | We can, in a week in any bog in Ireland
test: An oil tanker hits rocks near a coast. What will most likely happen to seabirds there? | Oil coats and poisons them and many die | They will fly faster and further | Nothing at all happens | They will find much more food in the oil
test: Why did Britain once have so many factories in towns near coal mines? | Coal powered the Industrial Revolution | Coal smelled nice | Coal made the towns warm even in summer | Coal kept the rain off the factory roofs
test: Why do sun and wind count as clean energy and coal does not? | Sun and wind do not burn fuel or release carbon dioxide | Sun and wind are made from coal | Coal is cleaner but more expensive | Sun and wind are fossil fuels too
test: A bog in Ireland has brown, squashy, rotting plant layers that burn when dried. What is it? | Peat | Coal | Oil | Chalk
test: Which fuel did not exist before the Carboniferous swamps? | Most of the world's coal | Sunlight | Wind | Running water
test: Thick smog in a big city makes people cough. What is one main cause? | Burning coal and oil | Too many trees in the parks | Ocean waves on the coast | Rainbows after summer rain
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of fossil fuels? | They derive from the remains of plants and minute marine organisms interred over millions of years | They derive from the bones of dinosaurs melted by volcanic heat | They are produced annually by the action of rainfall | They are formed within factories from sand
exam: Identify the principal consequence of the combustion of fossil fuels for the climate. | The emission of carbon dioxide, the principal cause of present global warming | The emission of oxygen, which cools the atmosphere | The cessation of all rainfall | The enlargement of the polar ice sheets
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the designation of fossil fuels as non-renewable. | Their formation required millions of years whereas their consumption occupies centuries | They are restored in full on an annual basis | Their supply is unlimited in perpetuity | They may lawfully be employed but once
exam: Ascertain the year in which the last coal-fired power station in Britain was closed. | Two thousand and twenty-four | Eighteen hundred and forty-two | Nineteen hundred and twenty-four | Two thousand and fifty
